  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThis morning...Satellite imagery shows low clouds extending from\nthe coastal waters to the low deserts. A weak wave undercutting\nthe dynamic ridge currently over SoCal is likely producing a low-\nlevel circulation which is bringing low clouds from the south into\nthe Borrego Desert and the Coachella valley. Radar shows a cluster\nof weak echoes nearly stationary over the coastal waters. These\nare light showers. High-resolution short-range models show these \nechoes (light showers) remaining nearly stationary until Monday \nwhen a low pressure system moves in from the northwest.\n\nAhead of this system a transient (dynamic) ridge will bring fair\nweather and slight warming for today, although scattered low\nclouds will persist. Daytime highs today will be mostly about 5\ndegrees higher than yesterday.\n\nThe first low pressure system will bring another round of widespread\nand organized precipitation. Ahead of the main band of rain and \nshowers, light precipitation could begin as early as Monday morning.\nThe main band of precipitation will move through SoCal from northwest\nto southeast Monday night/Tuesday Morning, moving off to the east\nby late Tuesday afternoon. As the upper level trough axis \napproaches, enhanced instability and dynamic forcing could produce\nsome isolated thunderstorms late Monday/early Tuesday. Snow \nlevels will likely remain above 7,000 ft through Tue morning \nbefore dropping to around 6,500 ft Tuesday afternoon. By then, \nmost of the precip will have moved east. This system will not have\naccess to large amounts of precipitable moisture. As a result, \ncurrent rainfall estimates range from 0.10-0.25 inch in the \ncoastal areas and valleys to between 0.25 and 0.40 inch on the \ncoastal mtn slopes. Most of the snowfall will remain above 7,000 \nft and accumulations are unlikely to exceed about 1 inch. Onshore \nflow begins to strengthen on Monday then peaks late Tuesday \nmorning, with southwest to west winds gusting 35-40 mph in the \nmtns and adjacent desert slopes. This will not be a particularly \ncold system and daytime high temperatures on Tue will be similar \nto what they were yesterday...remaining in the 60s west of the \nmtns, the 50s in the high desert and the 70s in the lower desert. \nTemperatures in the mtns will range from the 40s in San Diego \nCounty to the 20s in the higher elevations of the San Bernardino \nand San Jacinto Mtns.\n\nAnother brief period of fair and (slightly) warmer weather on Wed\nunder a weak transient ridge sandwiched between low pressure\nsystems. Daytime high temps will likely still be 5-10 degrees\nbelow normal in the inland valleys and as much as 10-15 degrees\nbelow normal in the mtns.\n\nAlthough the timing and track of the next system are still \nsomewhat uncertain, it does seem likely that we will get more \nunsettled weather for Thursday into Saturday. Colder conditions \nwith widespread precipitation is likely for late Thursday and \nFriday. This system does seem to have tighter pressure gradients\nso is likely to produce stronger westerly winds than the\npreceding event. Winds will likely peak Thu night/Fri morning. \nThe system will move east on Sat with precip tapering off Saturday\nmorning.",
